16. Stage-data separation and current scope

Every stage stores authority partitions separately:
- immutable accepted agentData;
- versioned humanData;
- recalculated derivedData;
- accepted data-change IDs;
- accepted control-event IDs;
- controlling prompt identity;
- current gate state;
- invalidation reference;
- revisions.

The renderer may show a combined view.
Storage MUST NOT merge human or derived values into immutable accepted agent data.

Every canonical record receives application-selected current-scope dimensions applicable to that record, including:
- input version;
- source-set version;
- requirements version;
- test-suite version;
- instruction version;
- iteration ID;
- candidate ID;
- run ID;
- baseline ID;
- product ID.

The agent cannot select or override scope.
Historical records remain preserved but cannot satisfy current gates.

16.1 Verification phase and due-stage model

Every release-bearing proposition, proof obligation, test, observation obligation, regression, and deferred failure test declares:

- VERIFICATION_PHASE: PREPRODUCT_ITERATION, FINAL_PRODUCT_DETERMINISTIC, FINAL_PRODUCT_MEANING, FINAL_PRODUCT_ADVERSARIAL, FINAL_REPRESENTATION, DELIVERY_IDENTITY, EVIDENCE_CLOSURE, REGISTRY_CLOSURE, or TERMINAL_DELIVERY;
- EARLIEST_EXECUTABLE_STAGE;
- REQUIRED_BY_STAGE;
- PER_RUN_REQUIRED;
- FINAL_PRODUCT_REQUIRED;
- DELIVERY_REQUIRED;
- TARGET_AVAILABILITY_CONDITION.

The application derives DUE_NOW only when the current stage is at least EARLIEST_EXECUTABLE_STAGE, the target-availability condition is TRUE, the obligation is current and applicable, and the current stage is at or beyond its REQUIRED_BY_STAGE. UNKNOWN target availability blocks only when the obligation could be due.

Stages 12, 13, 18, and 19 evaluate only current applicable PREPRODUCT_ITERATION obligations whose PER_RUN_REQUIRED value is TRUE and that are due by the relevant stage. Final-product, representation, delivery, evidence-closure, registry-closure, and terminal-delivery obligations do not block Stage 18 or Stage 19 before their targets exist, and Stages 12, 13, 18, and 19 MUST NOT require product-only, representation-only, delivery-only, or terminal-only propositions before their targets exist. They remain preserved as future required obligations and become due at their declared stages.

A deferred target-dependent test becomes due no earlier than the first stage where its target exists and no later than REQUIRED_BY_STAGE. It is never required before the target exists and is never represented as executed before actual execution.

16.2 Canonical-family sole-authority rule

Canonical record families are the sole substantive authority for canonical project data. Stage-data fields that correspond to canonical records may contain only: (a) pre-acceptance proposal payloads, (b) immutable canonical ID references, or (c) application-recomputed summaries. Duplicated substantive canonical values in stage data are prohibited. Any summary/reference mismatch fails integrity validation and cannot be resolved by choosing whichever copy is convenient.

REF-34e6870b8a1efd66fc2fd76bc08d0d554e2b.text:
14. Authority and ownership

Exactly four producer classes exist:
- HUMAN
- HUMAN_DECISION
- AGENT
- APPLICATION

For every complete field set:
union(all producer partitions) = complete field set
intersection(any two partitions) = empty
unclassified fields = 0

Runtime authority MUST NOT be inferred from:
- field-name suffixes;
- regular expressions;
- defaults;
- editable state;
- "all remaining fields belong to the agent."

One shared mutation-authorization rule must govern:
- response ingestion;
- human-input changes;
- human decisions;
- import validation;
- controlled correction;
- migration;
- native deterministic execution;
- application-derived commits.

14.1 Application authority

Application-owned facts include:
- canonical IDs;
- event IDs;
- event sequence;
- versions;
- byte sizes from actual bytes;
- SHA-256 from actual bytes;
- project revision;
- lifecycle state;
- current stage;
- current state;
- next required action;
- blocker identities and counts;
- coverage;
- verification-matrix completeness;
- exactly-ten calculations;
- current-scope selection;
- independence where observable;
- evidence sufficiency where objectively determinable;
- contradiction sets;
- stability metrics;
- convergence;
- baseline validity;
- release determination;
- artifact identity comparison;
- evidence-chain completeness;
- downstream invalidation;
- native deterministic observations;
- native deterministic results.

14.2 Human authority

Human-owned facts include:
- verbatim project request;
- supplied materials;
- explicit constraints;
- user-supplied authority;
- allowed or required tools;
- prohibited actions;
- answers to genuinely human-only questions.

14.3 Human-decision authority

Human-decision fields include:
- genuine intent confirmation;
- genuine tradeoff decisions;
- candidate-component selection;
- baseline authorization;
- delivery authorization;
- controlled exceptions;
- corrections to human-authority facts.

14.4 Agent authority

Agent-owned content includes:
- substantive proposals;
- semantic classifications;
- source findings;
- requirement language;
- test-design content;
- production-instruction content;
- review findings;
- root-cause explanations;
- meaning observations;
- adversarial findings;
- evidence claims.

An agent may submit evidence concerning an application-calculated proposition. It may not set the application calculation.

